\section{Analysis}\label{sec:analysis}

The five-way comparison (\S\ref{sec:experiments}) yields a paradox: training succeeds but inference fails, and no single architectural factor explains the gap. This section resolves it through a causal exclusion chain---each subsection kills one alternative hypothesis: \S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning} kills ``turn conditioning rescues late-turn attention''; \S\ref{subsec:pcgrad} kills ``gradient conflict prevents GAR convergence''; \S\ref{subsec:dist-shift} identifies exposure bias as the surviving explanation. \S\ref{subsec:mechanism-specificity} confirms selectivity via an orthogonal metric, and \S\ref{subsec:cross-model} confirms generalization across architectures.

\subsection{Gate Turn Conditioning}\label{subsec:gate-conditioning}

We compare gate values at turn~1 (early) and turn~21 (late) across all 28 layers. The gates invert the design intent: in 27/28 layers, gate values are \emph{lower} at turn~1 than turn~21 (Wilcoxon $p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$, $r = 0.975$)---e.g., layer~23 shifts from 0.769 to 0.979. NTP pressure pushes gates toward 1.0, yielding inference-time values of 0.93--0.98 (2--7\% attenuation; Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). The Uniform Gate learns comparable per-layer variation (L3\,=\,0.777, L27\,=\,1.000) despite lacking turn conditioning, confirming the structure arises from LoRA$+$GAR pressure (Figure~\ref{fig:gate-shift}a). Does the NTP-GAR gradient conflict prevent the intended behavior?

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{fig3_gate_shift.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gate turn conditioning: per-layer gate values at turn~1 vs turn~21. In 27/28 layers, gates approach 1.0 at late turns, inverting the design intent ($p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$). (b)~Distribution shift: the TF-AR GAR gap persists across all five variants (TG, +PCGrad, UG, LoRA-only, Base) under our evaluation conditions.}
  \label{fig:gate-shift}
\end{figure}

\subsection{PCGrad Gradient Conflict Exclusion}\label{subsec:pcgrad}

NTP and GAR gradients oppose in 68--79\% of layers (cosine similarity $-$0.089$\to$$-$0.042 across epochs), with NTP magnitude dominance growing from 7.5$\times$ to 14.7$\times$. If this conflict prevents GAR convergence, eliminating it via PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}---projecting conflicting gradients onto the other's normal plane---should prevent the collapse. To our knowledge, this is the first application of PCGrad as a \emph{diagnostic} tool rather than an optimization method.

PCGrad shifts cosine similarity to oscillate around zero, yet TF metrics are unchanged. AR GAR \emph{worsens}: 0.064$\to$0.030 ($p$\,=\,0.002; Figure~\ref{fig:training-pcgrad}b). By \emph{modus tollens}: removing direction conflict does not prevent collapse; therefore it is not the cause. The 7.5--14.7$\times$ magnitude dominance renders directional interference inconsequential.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth,height=0.42\textheight,keepaspectratio]{fig2_training_pcgrad.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gradient cosine similarity between NTP and GAR objectives. ThreadGate remains consistently negative; +PCGrad oscillates around zero. (b)~IID GAR and PPL are unchanged; AR GAR worsens (0.064$\to$0.030), ruling out gradient conflict as the cause.}
  \label{fig:training-pcgrad}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Distribution Shift Analysis}\label{subsec:dist-shift}

The TF-AR GAR gap persists across all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). GAR-Dongre provides a control: it remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) with no distribution gap, so the shift selectively destroys concentrated last-token attention while leaving diffuse attention unaffected. The mechanism is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled,zhang2019bridging,arora2022exposure}: during training the model conditions on ground-truth prefixes; at inference, on its own outputs. NTP self-recovers \citep{he2019exposure} because prediction is the same task at both regimes; GAR optimizes a distributional property with no autoregressive counterpart. Attention patterns over model-generated tokens have no training-time analogue, explaining why TF GAR-ours remains near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 (7--8\% decay; Figure~\ref{fig:attention-heatmap}) while AR GAR collapses (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}a).

\subsection{Mechanism Specificity}\label{subsec:mechanism-specificity}

We test specificity with Turn-level Residual Coherence (TRC), the cosine similarity between adjacent-turn hidden states. TF/AR TRC is 0.334/0.332 for ThreadGate (gap $+$0.002) and 0.349/0.411 for LoRA-only (gap $-$0.062, reversed), confirming exposure bias disrupts a specific routing behavior while leaving representations intact. Cross-model evaluation confirms: GAR-Dongre shows negligible shift on both Llama (1.002$\times$) and Qwen (1.28$\times$) vs.\ the order-of-magnitude GAR-ours collapse.

\subsection{Cross-Model Replication}\label{subsec:cross-model}

We replicate on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \citep{grattafiori2024llama3} (Base, ThreadGate, LoRA-only; $N$\,=\,20 conversations, 4 turns each). TF GAR-ours is 0.484 (TG) and 0.481 (LoRA-only); gating contributes zero benefit, consistent with Qwen. The AR distribution is heavy-tailed (CoV\,$>$\,100\%): ThreadGate shows a 5\% spike ratio (2/40 turns with GAR\,$\geq$\,0.4, std\,=\,0.100) while LoRA-only has 0\% spikes (std\,=\,0.048). We therefore report medians: 0.018 for both TG and LoRA-only (Base: 0.009), yielding TF-AR gaps of 27$\times$ and 26$\times$ (Figure~\ref{fig:crossmodel}a). Mean GAR-ours is 0.043 (TG) and 0.028 (LoRA-only), gaps of 11$\times$ and 17$\times$---the mean-median divergence confirms that rare high-GAR turns inflate means without reflecting typical model behavior (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-llama}).

Gate profiles differ qualitatively across architectures (Figure~\ref{fig:crossmodel}b): Qwen attenuates early layers (L1\,=\,0.793, L5\,=\,0.797) while Llama shows a V-shaped valley at output-adjacent layers (L25\,=\,0.719), suggesting that the location of maximum gate attenuation is architecture-dependent---early-middle for Qwen, late-middle for Llama---but the convergence toward identity under NTP pressure is universal. Both architectures share the same outcome: gates learn per-layer variation during training but are driven toward passivity at inference, where NTP dominates. The gap is substantial on both architectures (8--17$\times$ on Qwen, 27$\times$ on Llama), consistent with exposure bias as an architecture-independent phenomenon.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_crossmodel.pdf}
  \caption{Cross-architecture validation. (a)~The TF-AR gap persists on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ by median), exceeding Qwen's 8$\times$. (b)~Gate profiles on normalized depth: Qwen dips at early layers, Llama at late layers---architecture-dependent patterns, but both converge toward identity.}
  \label{fig:crossmodel}
\end{figure}

